{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Amazon RDS and Aurora now support R8g and M8g database instances in additional AWS Regions - AWS","context":"Amazon RDS and Aurora now support R8g and M8g database instances in additional AWS Regions. R8g instances are available for Amazon Aurora (MySQL and PostgreSQL compatibility) and Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL, MySQL, and MariaDB in Asia Pacific (Hyderabad, Melbourne, Malaysia), Europe (London, Paris, Zurich), AWS GovCloud (US-East), South America (Sao Paulo), and Mexico (Central) regions. M8g instances are supported for Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL, MySQL, and MariaDB in US West (N. California), Asia Pacific (Mumbai, Sydney, Hong Kong, Seoul, Malaysia, Singapore), Canada West (Calgary), Europe (Zurich, Milan, Paris), South America (Sao Paulo), and Africa (Cape Town) regions. R8g instances provide up to 40% performance improvement and up to 29% price/performance improvement over Graviton3-based instances, with up to 192 vCPUs, an 8:1 ratio of memory to vCPU, up to 50Gbps enhanced networking bandwidth, and up to 40Gbps of bandwidth to Amazon Elastic Block Store (Amazon EBS). M8g instances offer similar benefits. These instances are built on the AWS Nitro System and can be launched through the Amazon RDS Management Console or AWS Command Line Interface. For specific engine versions and pricing information, refer to the Aurora and RDS documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/7/amazon-rds-aurora-r8g-m8g-regions/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Amazon Aurora and RDS for PostgreSQL, MySQL and MariaDB now support r8g, r7g, and r7i database instances in additional AWS Regions - AWS","context":"Amazon Aurora and Relational Database Service (RDS) for PostgreSQL, MySQL, and MariaDB now support new database instance types, r8g, r7g, and r7i, in additional AWS regions. R8g instances are available in Asia Pacific (Hong Kong, Osaka, Jakarta, Seoul, Singapore, and Canada Central), R7i in Asia Pacific (Hyderabad), and R7g in Africa (Cape Town). These instances are based on AWS Graviton4 processors and offer up to 40% performance improvement and up to 29% price/performance improvement over Graviton3-based instances. R8g instances come in 24xlarge and 48xlarge sizes, delivering up to 192 vCPUs, an 8:1 ratio of memory to vCPU with DDR5 memory, enhanced networking bandwidth of up to 50Gbps, and up to 40Gbps bandwidth to Amazon Elastic Block Store. R7g instances offer up to 128 vCPUs and up to 384GB of memory. R8g and R7g instances are available for Amazon Aurora with MySQL compatibility and Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL, MySQL, and MariaDB. For specific engine versions and pricing information, refer to the Aurora and RDS documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/01/amazon-aurora-rds-r8g-r7g-r7i-instances-additional-regions/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL, MySQL, and MariaDB now supports M9g database instances - AWS","context":"Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL, MySQL, and MariaDB now supports M9g database instances, which are powered by AWS Graviton5 processors and are generally available. These instances offer up to a 30% performance improvement and up to a 23% price/performance improvement over Graviton4-based instances of equivalent sizes. M9g DB instances are available in new 24xlarge and 48xlarge sizes, providing up to 192 vCPU, up to 100Gbps enhanced networking bandwidth, and up to 72Gbps of bandwidth to Amazon EBS. The instances are available in US East (N. Virginia, Ohio), US West (Oregon), and Europe (Frankfurt) Regions. M9g DB instances offer significant improvements in performance and cost efficiency","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/06/amazon-rds-postgresql-mysql-mariadb-m9g-instances/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"rds-sqlserver","skill_description":"Provides connectivity, authentication, and troubleshooting guidance for Amazon RDS for SQL Server. Applicable when users ask about SSMS times out connecting from EC2, Cannot generate SSPI context with Windows auth, connect RDS SQL Server from Lambda with pymssql, auth_scheme shows NTLM instead of KERBEROS on ECS Fargate, SSM tunnel to RDS SQL Server from laptop, port 1433 security group, TrustServerCertificate=True for localhost tunnels, SPN MSSQLSvc, AWS Managed Microsoft AD, CNAME not RDS endpoint for Kerberos, tds_version='7.4', encryption='require', port-as-string for pymssql, Secrets Manager credential caching in Lambda, error 18456 login failed. Covers Python (pymssql, pyodbc), .NET (Microsoft.Data.SqlClient), Java (JDBC mssql-jdbc), Node.js (tedious), IAM auth via RDS Proxy, and VPC/ECS/EKS/Lambda deployment.\n\nServices: rds, secretsmanager, ec2, lambda, ecs, eks, directoryservice\nTasks: connect, configure, troubleshoot, deploy, authenticate, create, provision, modify\nPersona: developer, devops, dba\nWorkload: application, legacy-migration, .net, java, python, nodejs\n*","skill_name":"rds-sqlserver"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"rds-oss","skill_description":"Advises on Amazon RDS open-source engines (MySQL, MariaDB, PostgreSQL) for instance creation, upgrade planning, commitment pricing, proxy evaluation, and Blue/Green deployments. Handles any RDS MySQL, MariaDB, or PostgreSQL question, including create a production-ready RDS MySQL instance, provision an RDS PostgreSQL database, run the RDS upgrade advisor for my RDS MySQL instance, what are my upgrade options, upgrade RDS MariaDB from 10.6 to the latest version, should I buy reserved instances or a savings plan for db.r7g.2xlarge RDS MySQL, change a VARCHAR to INT column on RDS MySQL 8.0 with Blue/Green, and does RDS Proxy help when PgBouncer already runs in transaction mode. Covers instance creation with production best practices, describe-db-instances and describe-db-engine-versions upgrade-target workflow, live prechecks via SSM or direct connection, RI versus DSP commitment pricing, RDS Proxy versus PgBouncer, and Blue/Green lifecycle with binlog replay compatibility.\n\nServices: rds, mysql, mariadb, postgres, rds-proxy, savings-plans\nTasks: advise, analyze, optimize, create, provision, upgrade-planning, commitment-analysis, schema-change\nPersona: developer, devops, architect, dba, finance\nWorkload: database, relational-database\n*","skill_name":"rds-oss"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify whether RDS for SQL Server supports Graviton instances"}

