{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"What's New at AWS - Cloud Innovation & News","context":"Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (EC2) C7g instances, which are powered by AWS Graviton3 processors, are now available in three additional regions: Middle East (Bahrain), Africa (Cape Town), and Asia Pacific (Jakarta). These instances offer up to 25% better compute performance than AWS Graviton2 processors and are built on the AWS Nitro System. They also use up to 60% less energy than comparable EC2 instances, reducing cloud carbon footprint. C7g instances come in 9 different sizes, including bare metal, and provide up to 30 Gbps networking bandwidth and up to 20 Gbps of bandwidth to the Amazon Elastic Block Store (EBS). To learn more about these instances, users can refer to the Amazon EC2 C7g page. AWS provides resources such as the AWS Graviton Fast Start program and Porting Advisor for Graviton to help migrate workloads to Graviton-based instances. Users can get started by using the AWS Management Console","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/08/amazon-ec2-c7g-instances-in-regions/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"What's New at AWS - Cloud Innovation & News","context":"Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (EC2) R7g instances, which are powered by AWS Graviton3 processors, are now available in the AWS Africa (Cape Town) region. These instances offer up to 25% better compute performance than AWS Graviton2 processors and are built on the AWS Nitro System. They also use up to 60% less energy than comparable EC2 instances, reducing cloud carbon footprint. R7g instances are available in 9 different sizes, including bare metal, and offer up to 30 Gbps networking bandwidth and up to 20 Gbps of bandwidth to the Amazon Elastic Block Store (EBS). To learn more about these instances, users can refer to the Amazon EC2 R7g page. AWS provides resources such as the AWS Graviton Fast Start program and Porting Advisor for Graviton to help migrate workloads to Graviton-based instances. To get started, users can use the AWS Management Console","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/08/amazon-ec2-r7g-instances-africa-cape-town/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Amazon EC2 C7gd instances are now available in additional AWS Regions - AWS","context":"Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (EC2) C7gd instances, which offer up to 3.8 TB of local NVMe-based SSD block-level storage and are powered by Graviton3 processors with DDR5 memory, are now available in the Europe (Zurich) Region. These instances, which are built on the AWS Nitro System, are ideal for applications requiring high-speed, low latency local storage, such as those utilizing temporary storage for scratch space, temporary files, and caches. The C7gd instances offer up to 45% improved real-time NVMe storage performance compared to comparable Graviton2-based instances. Additionally, Graviton3-based instances consume up to 60% less energy for the same performance level, enabling users to decrease their carbon footprint in the cloud. To learn more about C7gd instances, users can refer to the Amazon C7gd Instances page. For assistance with migrating workloads to Graviton-based instances, users can explore the AWS Graviton Fast Start program and Porting Advisor for Graviton. To get started, users can utilize the AWS Management Console","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/10/amazon-ec2-c7gd-instances-additional-aws-regions/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"aws-compute","skill_description":"Provisions, scales, and operates Amazon EC2 virtual-machine workloads: instance-type selection (Graviton/Arm64, burstable T credits, GPU, instance store vs EBS), launch templates, Auto Scaling groups (scaling policies, instance refresh, mixed instances, Spot, warm pools, lifecycle hooks), IMDSv2, placement groups, Elastic IPs, AMI lifecycle, and Systems Manager fleet operations (Session Manager, Run Command, Patch Manager). Applies to EC2 instance and fleet questions, InsufficientInstanceCapacity, CPU-credit/surplus charges, IMDSv2 401s, instances stuck in Pending:Wait, ASG not replacing unhealthy instances, status-check failures, SSH refused/timed out, or instances missing as SSM managed nodes. For a single secure instance launch, the launching-ec2-instance-with-best-practices skill is more appropriate; for instance profiles, see setting-up-ec2-instance-profiles; for Image Builder, see amazon-ec2-image-builder. Does NOT cover Lambda, ECS/Fargate, EKS, VPC/ALB/NLB design, or IAM policy authoring.\n\nServices: ec2, autoscaling, ssm, elasticloadbalancing\nTasks: provision, scale, operate, debug, optimize\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: compute\n*","skill_name":"aws-compute"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"aws-billing-and-cost-management","skill_description":"Analyze AWS costs, find savings, manage budgets, evaluate Savings Plans and Reserved Instances, right-size EC2/Lambda/RDS/EBS with Compute Optimizer, look up service pricing, query CUR with Athena, detect cost anomalies, scope costs to billing views, and monitor Free Tier usage. Triggers on: AWS bill, cost analysis, reduce spend, savings plan, reserved instance, right-size, budget alert, cost optimization, pricing, free tier, cost anomaly, CUR, cost audit, billing view, billing view ARN.\n\n\nServices: cost-explorer, budgets, compute-optimizer, cost-optimization-hub, pricing, savings-plans, cur\nTasks: analyze-cost, optimize, budget, right-size, pricing-lookup, audit\nPersona: developer, finops-practitioner, cloud-engineer\nWorkload: cost-management\n*","skill_name":"aws-billing-and-cost-management"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify AWS's published energy efficiency claim for Graviton processors"}
